WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.049
Film Courage: When a writer brings their work
to you or even hearing from agents who’ve

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:10.860
had a writer bring work to them, what sort
of telltale signs appear when it’s so deeply

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.580
personal and you can tell that this writer
does not have enough perspective?

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:21.100
Whereas when you can maybe tell that it is
loosely based on a multitude of people and

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:27.539
there’s a little more definition to the
problem or maybe there is not and I’m projecting

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:28.539
this?

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:30.670
Erik Bork, Author/Screenwriter: I don’t
know if there are telltale signs because you

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:35.150
never know when you’re reading something
is this the author’s own life story? I mean

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:41.750
you can suspect it but I would say the absence
of some of the elements we’re talking about

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:47.080
(the seven elements of what the problem) the
absence of those is the main thing that a

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:53.720
reader is noticing. That it isn’t that entertaining
to watch or the stakes aren’t life-altering

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.510
(it doesn’t seem like). Or the character
isn’t relatable, the problem isn’t punishing

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:03.710
enough or what is going on isn’t believable
enough. Those kind of things are what we’re

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:10.420
just noticing right away. Once we notice them
we may speculate Oh this is because it’s

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:15.420
their own true story and their own life doesn’t
have those elements but we don’t really

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:16.680
know for sure.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.500
So I don’t really know if I can say I can
tell this is a true-person personal story

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.920
unless I really know the writer and it’s
pretty obvious that it’s their life. It’s

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.870
more the absence of some of those elements
not appearing as strongly as we would want

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:38.200
makes us less enthused about the script. And
it may or may not be because it was a personal

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.200
story like that.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:45.690
Film Courage: Do you remember the first feedback you
received from a third party that these elements

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:46.690
needed to be more pronounced?

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:47.690
Erik: Oh yeah.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:48.690
Film Courage: How was that for you?

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:53.100
Erik: Devastating, terrible. The worst moment
in my life up to that point. So I guess I

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.120
had a pretty good life if the worst moment
in my life is having someone tear apart a

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:03.830
script. But some of the early script that
I wrote I would probably cringe at now and

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:07.930
the stuff I write now I’ll probably cringe
at 6 months from now. It’s not an easy field

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:11.670
is what I’m saying. You won’t always have
perspective on what you’re writing yourself

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:14.340
and whether others are going to respond well
to it.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:19.230
But I’ve had people say that there is just
not enough conflict (it needs more conflict)

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:25.220
and I in my early days thought conflict meant
like people arguing, fighting or car chases.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:31.470
I didn’t really know what that meant exactly
and so I didn’t write scripts and I didn’t

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:36.209
write movies that people constantly arguing
or fighting or car chasing. So I thought I

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:40.670
was writing something that was the kind of
stuff I would like. But a third party reading

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:44.900
it said you know you did some good things
hear I just thought it needed more conflict.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.070
I didn’t really know what they meant. Now
after doing it for years and reading a lot

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:52.930
of scripts, I know what they meant. To me
conflict is synonymous with problem.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:59.300
So it’s not that your character has to always
been in obvious conflict with people and expressing

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:05.270
and fighting. It’s that they have to be
in conflict with their situation and they

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:10.760
have to have problems. Whenever your character
doesn’t have a problem or isn’t even actively

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:15.040
struggling against or trying to resolve a
problem, the audience tends to check out a

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:22.099
bit, right? So that usually leads to conflict
with other characters trying to solve a problem

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:25.550
in life generally leads to some interpersonal
conflict. Because a lot of problems that are

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:30.629
big enough for a movie that we want to solve,
big enough for a TV show are problems that

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:35.099
all involve relations and interactions with
other people. So there will be conflict but

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:40.440
it’s not that limited version of what I
thought conflict meant when I got that critique.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:46.790
So the problem not being punishing enough
(letter P the first one in P R O B L E M in

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:51.080
my book) was probably the first thing that
I grappled with in my own stuff.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.530
People saying it’s just not enough conflict
meaning it’s just not a big enough problem,

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:59.410
it’s maybe not a life-altering enough problem
which is the L word which is the level of

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:03.270
the stakes of the problem. Why does it matter
that they solve this problem? Why does the

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:07.760
audience care that they solve it or not? Is
it big enough and is it relatable enough?

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.849
Which is the R word where the audience is
starting to feel like “It’s our problem,

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:16.310
too.” We are standing in the shoes of this
character as they try to solve this problem

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:19.769
and we feel something about it, we want it
to be solve because it’s kind of like it’s

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:24.580
us now, that emotional engagement with the
characters, that really key thing we’re

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:28.490
trying to achieve as writers is that it’s
not easy to achieve and sometimes writers

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:33.439
don’t see the things necessary to kind of
achieve that which is a lot of the kind of

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:34.439
relatable stuff.

00:04:34.439 --> 00:04:40.740
So my first critique of something not punishable
enough was probably also not big enough stakes,

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.080
why do people relate to this character? Because
you didn’t find that sort of universal hook

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:49.830
that pulled people in. The character didn’t
have those elements that make the audience

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:56.139
just start to feel like I’m bonded with
them which is such a key part.

00:04:56.139 --> 00:05:00.659
For me like the beginning of any screenplay
(which Save The Cat calls the setup section,

00:05:00.659 --> 00:05:06.819
the first ten pages), it’s all about achieving
that bonding with the audience and people

00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:10.490
often talk about people put down a script
in the first few pages if it’s not grabbing

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:16.580
them and writers tend to think “Oh you grab
them by having really exciting spectacle in

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:22.259
the beginning with really high stakes, with
action, or entertainment value, which is always

00:05:22.259 --> 00:05:27.300
helpful but to me the more important goal
in those first ten pages is to introduce your

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:33.099
main character at some length and at some
breadth and at some depth so the audience

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:37.960
starts to see what the key things are that
would make us care about them. What makes

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:43.659
them a compelling, relatable person? Even
before the main story problem has kicked in.

00:05:43.659 --> 00:05:49.370
What makes us just kind of want to follow
them? In Save The Cat, they save a cat which

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:54.599
makes us like them because they do something
self-sacrificing and helpful. That’s sort

00:05:54.599 --> 00:05:59.119
of a semi-joke premise that Blake Snyder came
up with…just have your character save a

00:05:59.119 --> 00:06:03.279
cat. I think what he was really getting at
was a really vital thing that writers tend

00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:08.779
to sometimes bypass or not understand which
is give us a reason to bond with that character

00:06:08.779 --> 00:06:12.280
before your story really kicks in.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:17.279
So I probably failed in all those and other
ways in my early scripts and maybe some later

00:06:17.279 --> 00:06:58.289
scripts that made people go “Ahhhh…needs
more conflict.”

